analysis:
Examined units: StartCommand+Modes.swift base/head, UpdateCommand.swift metadata, patch, base and head, and the metadata for the added file UpdateRollback.swift. StartCommand+Modes.swift changes only the wording of the auto-update quarantine message at line 413. Startup auto-update control flow is unchanged.

UpdateCommand.swift adds `--rollback` and `-y/--yes` flags (head 44-48). validate() (head 50-57) rejects rollback combined with --check-only, --override-quarantine or --coordinator. It also rejects --yes without --rollback. run() dispatches to UpdateRollback before the check and update paths (head 69-76). The SelfUpdater is built from the config coordinator URL. The --override-quarantine flag now also covers rollback quarantine; only its help text and warnings changed (head 40, 117-118, 144).

Security relevance: rollback is a deliberate, operator-initiated local downgrade to the predecessor release. The operator is already ADV-001 with full OS access (TB-003, T-013). They could install any binary, so local rollback adds no new capability against the coordinator. Serving authorization for the restored build still depends on these platform-owned contracts (TB-011, T-054, A-011, A-017):
- coordinator binary-hash registry checks;
- App Attest qualification;
- revocation.
This PR does not visibly change those controls.

The rollback reuses the watchdog's journaled predecessor rollback (T-043, per the excerpted doc comment). It also reuses the drain/update-lease and restart path.

Coverage limit: the UpdateRollback.swift body (173 lines) was not supplied beyond a truncated patch excerpt. The following could not be verified:
- confirmation and canAsk handling;
- the cluster refusal;
- foreground vs service handling;
- the update-lease ordering of drain → swap → restart;
- quarantine marking of the installed release;
- whether the restored predecessor's integrity is re-verified before exec;
- watchdog rearm.

The next pass should examine UpdateRollback.swift and SelfUpdater.rollBackToPredecessor for:
- TOCTOU or journaling issues;
- a lease held across restart;
- failure paths that leave a half-swapped layout or a disabled watchdog;
- whether quarantine state could be abused to block legitimate updates.
The last is an availability concern only, and only by the local operator.

No regression identified in the visible evidence.
